{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T22:53:22.301Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_wlUQRMnPRLsCauhPtVR3YNSL","process_id":"57988","turn_id":"019db23f-4ab9-7890-84ed-d8686c4395d3","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/.codex/superpowers/skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/.codex/superpowers/skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/.codex/superpowers/skills/brainstorming/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: brainstorming\ndescription: \"You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\"\n---\n\n# Brainstorming Ideas Into Designs\n\nHelp turn ideas into fully formed designs and specs through natural collaborative dialogue.\n\nStart by understanding the current project context, then ask questions one at a time to refine the idea. Once you understand what you're building, present the design and get user approval.\n\n<HARD-GATE>\nDo NOT invoke any implementation skill, write any code, scaffold any project, or take any implementation action until you have presented a design and the user has approved it. This applies to EVERY project regardless of perceived simplicity.\n</HARD-GATE>\n\n## Anti-Pattern: \"This Is Too Simple To Need A Design\"\n\nEvery project goes through this process. A todo list, a single-function utility, a config change — all of them. \"Simple\" projects are where unexamined assumptions cause the most wasted work. The design can be short (a few sentences for truly simple projects), but you MUST present it and get approval.\n\n## Checklist\n\nYou MUST create a task for each of these items and complete them in order:\n\n1. **Explore project context** — check files, docs, recent commits\n2. **Offer visual companion** (if topic will involve visual questions) — this is its own message, not combined with a clarifying question. See the Visual Companion section below.\n3. **Ask clarifying questions** — one at a time, understand purpose/constraints/success criteria\n4. **Propose 2-3 approaches** — with trade-offs and your recommendation\n5. **Present design** — in sections scaled to their complexity, get user approval after each section\n6. **Write design doc** — save to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md` and commit\n7. **Spec self-review** — quick inline check for placeholders, contradictions, ambiguity, scope (see below)\n8. **User reviews written spec** — ask user to review the spec file before proceeding\n9. **Transition to implementation** — invoke writing-plans skill to create implementation plan\n\n## Process Flow\n\n```dot\ndigraph brainstorming {\n    \"Explore project context\" [shape=box];\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" [shape=box];\n    \"Ask clarifying questions\" [shape=box];\n    \"Propose 2-3 approaches\" [shape=box];\n    \"Present design sections\" [shape=box];\n    \"User approves design?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Write design doc\" [shape=box];\n    \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\" [shape=box];\n    \"User reviews spec?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Invoke writing-plans skill\" [shape=doublecircle];\n\n    \"Explore project context\" -> \"Visual questions ahead?\";\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" -> \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" -> \"Ask clarifying questions\" [label=\"no\"];\n    \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" -> \"Ask clarifying questions\";\n    \"Ask clarifying questions\" -> \"Propose 2-3 approaches\";\n    \"Propose 2-3 approaches\" -> \"Present design sections\";\n    \"Present design sections\" -> \"User approves design?\";\n    \"User approves design?\" -> \"Present design sections\" [label=\"no, revise\"];\n    \"User approves design?\" -> \"Write design doc\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Write design doc\" -> \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\";\n    \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\" -> \"User reviews spec?\";\n    \"User reviews spec?\" -> \"Write design doc\" [label=\"changes requested\"];\n    \"User reviews spec?\" -> \"Invoke writing-plans skill\" [label=\"approved\"];\n}\n```\n\n**The terminal state is invoking writing-plans.** Do NOT invoke frontend-design, mcp-builder, or any other implementation skill. The ONLY skill you invoke after brainstorming is writing-plans.\n\n## The Process\n\n**Understanding the idea:**\n\n- Check out the current project state first (files, docs, recent commits)\n- Before asking detailed questions, assess scope: if the request describes multiple independent subsystems (e.g., \"build a platform with chat, file storage, billing, and analytics\"), flag this immediately. Don't spend questions refining details of a project that needs to be decomposed first.\n- If the project is too large for a single spec, help the user decompose into sub-projects: what are the independent pieces, how do they relate, what order should they be built? Then brainstorm the first sub-project through the normal design flow. Each sub-project gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle.\n- For appropriately-scoped projects, ask questions one at a time to refine the idea\n- Prefer multiple choice questions when possible, but open-ended is fine too\n- Only one question per message - if a topic needs more exploration, break it into multiple questions\n- Focus on understanding: purpose, constraints, success criteria\n\n**Exploring approaches:**\n\n- Propose…634 chars truncated…smaller units that each have one clear purpose, communicate through well-defined interfaces, and can be understood and tested independently\n- For each unit, you should be able to answer: what does it do, how do you use it, and what does it depend on?\n- Can someone understand what a unit does without reading its internals? Can you change the internals without breaking consumers? If not, the boundaries need work.\n- Smaller, well-bounded units are also easier for you to work with - you reason better about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. When a file grows large, that's often a signal that it's doing too much.\n\n**Working in existing codebases:**\n\n- Explore the current structure before proposing changes. Follow existing patterns.\n- Where existing code has problems that affect the work (e.g., a file that's grown too large, unclear boundaries, tangled responsibilities), include targeted improvements as part of the design - the way a good developer improves code they're working in.\n- Don't propose unrelated refactoring. Stay focused on what serves the current goal.\n\n## After the Design\n\n**Documentation:**\n\n- Write the validated design (spec) to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`\n  - (User preferences for spec location override this default)\n- Use elements-of-style:writing-clearly-and-concisely skill if available\n- Commit the design document to git\n\n**Spec Self-Review:**\nAfter writing the spec document, look at it with fresh eyes:\n\n1. **Placeholder scan:** Any \"TBD\", \"TODO\", incomplete sections, or vague requirements? Fix them.\n2. **Internal consistency:** Do any sections contradict each other? Does the architecture match the feature descriptions?\n3. **Scope check:** Is this focused enough for a single implementation plan, or does it need decomposition?\n4. **Ambiguity check:** Could any requirement be interpreted two different ways? If so, pick one and make it explicit.\n\nFix any issues inline. No need to re-review — just fix and move on.\n\n**User Review Gate:**\nAfter the spec review loop passes, ask the user to review the written spec before proceeding:\n\n> \"Spec written and committed to `<path>`. Please review it and let me know if you want to make any changes before we start writing out the implementation plan.\"\n\nWait for the user's response. If they request changes, make them and re-run the spec review loop. Only proceed once the user approves.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n- Invoke the writing-plans skill to create a detailed implementation plan\n- Do NOT invoke any other skill. writing-plans is the next step.\n\n## Key Principles\n\n- **One question at a time** - Don't overwhelm with multiple questions\n- **Multiple choice preferred** - Easier to answer than open-ended when possible\n- **YAGNI ruthlessly** - Remove unnecessary features from all designs\n- **Explore alternatives** - Always propose 2-3 approaches before settling\n- **Incremental validation** - Present design, get approval before moving on\n- **Be flexible** - Go back and clarify when something doesn't make sense\n\n## Visual Companion\n\nA browser-based companion for showing mockups, diagrams, and visual options during brainstorming. Available as a tool — not a mode. Accepting the companion means it's available for questions that benefit from visual treatment; it does NOT mean every question goes through the browser.\n\n**Offering the companion:** When you anticipate that upcoming questions will involve visual content (mockups, layouts, diagrams), offer it once for consent:\n> \"Some of what we're working on might be easier to explain if I can show it to you in a web browser. I can put together mockups, diagrams, comparisons, and other visuals as we go. This feature is still new and can be token-intensive. Want to try it? (Requires opening a local URL)\"\n\n**This offer MUST be its own message.** Do not combine it with clarifying questions, context summaries, or any other content. The message should contain ONLY the offer above and nothing else. Wait for the user's response before continuing. If they decline, proceed with text-only brainstorming.\n\n**Per-question decision:** Even after the user accepts, decide FOR EACH QUESTION whether to use the browser or the terminal. The test: **would the user understand this better by seeing it than reading it?**\n\n- **Use the browser** for content that IS visual — mockups, wireframes, layout comparisons, architecture diagrams, side-by-side visual designs\n- **Use the terminal** for content that is text — requirements questions, conceptual choices, tradeoff lists, A/B/C/D text options, scope decisions\n\nA question about a UI topic is not automatically a visual question. \"What does personality mean in this context?\" is a conceptual question — use the terminal. \"Which wizard layout works better?\" is a visual question — use the browser.\n\nIf they agree to the companion, read the detailed guide before proceeding:\n`skills/brainstorming/visual-companion.md`\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":4000},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T22:53:22.301Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_Cy6okC9VKfuz2xGpvxvUFdyM","process_id":"48339","turn_id":"019db23f-4ab9-7890-84ed-d8686c4395d3","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md","name":"rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/company-knowledge/rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\ntitle: Transcript Compaction\nstate: draft\nauthor: \"soph\"\ndate: 2026-02-26\nupdated: 2026-03-19\ntags: checkpoints, storage, github-action\n---\n\n## TL;DR\n\nSession transcripts on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are 96.5% larger than what the UI needs. We introduce:\n\n1. **Dual-write:** CLI writes both raw transcript (`full.jsonl`) and compact **Entire Transcript Format** (`transcript.jsonl`) at condensation time.\n2. **Split storage:** Permanent data (metadata + compact transcripts) goes on an append-only `/main` ref. Raw logs go on a separate `/full/current` ref that rotates into numbered archived generations when full. All stored under `refs/entire/` — invisible in GitHub's branch UI.\n3. **Cleanup = delete entire generations.** No tree surgery, no force-push, no history rewriting. Just delete the archived ref — all blobs become unreachable.\n\nOnly 2-3 active refs at any time. The CLI stays append-only. The API reads only the compact format.\n\n## Problem\n\nSession transcripts stored on `entire/checkpoints/v1` are significantly larger than what the UI needs. Claude Code's JSONL format includes streaming progress, subagent messages, thinking blocks, full tool output, and per-message metadata that the API parser never reads.\n\nAcross 10 real transcripts (158 MB total), **96.5% of the data is unnecessary for the UI**. Individual files ranged from 63% to 99.9% reduction. Heavy subagent sessions (common with Task tool usage) can be 46 MB raw but only 40 KB after stripping.\n\nAdditionally, transcripts grow over a session — when a user makes multiple commits, each checkpoint stores the full transcript up to that point. The second checkpoint contains everything from the first plus new lines. This duplication is intentional: `entire resume` needs the complete transcript to reconstruct conversation state, and storing the full log at each checkpoint means resume works from any single checkpoint without concatenating prior ones. But after the resume window passes, this duplication is pure waste.\n\n## Proposed Solution\n\n### Dual-write: raw + compact format\n\nRather than only storing agent-native transcripts and stripping them later, the CLI writes **two files** to **two separate refs** at condensation time:\n\n1. **`full.jsonl`** → written to the ephemeral `/full/current` ref. Raw agent transcript (unchanged). Used by `entire resume` to reconstruct conversation state. Deleted with its generation after the retention window.\n2. **`transcript.jsonl`** → written to the permanent `/main` ref. Compact **Entire Transcript Format**. Agent-agnostic, contains only what the UI/API needs. Never deleted.\n\nThis addresses several concerns from the original RFD review:\n\n- **Rome (API) only needs to understand one format** — not Claude, Cursor, Gemini, Codex, and Copilot transcripts individually (Georg's point). Agent-specific parsing stays in the CLI; the API reads only the Entire Transcript Format.\n- **No risky post-hoc transformation** — the compact format is written at recording time by the same CLI version that understands the agent's format. No version skew between writer and compactor.\n- **Cleanup becomes simple deletion** — raw logs live in separate, ephemeral refs. After the retention window, delete the entire ref — no tree surgery, no force-push.\n\n### Entire Transcript Format\n\nA normalized, agent-agnostic JSONL format. One line per conversational turn. Versioned so the API can handle format evolution.\n\nThe first line is a **header** declaring the format version and the source agent:\n\n```jsonl\n{\"v\":1,\"agent\":\"claude-code\",\"cli_version\":\"0.42.0\"}\n```\n\nSubsequent lines are conversational entries:\n\n```jsonl\n{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"user\",\"ts\":\"...\",\"content\":\"user message text\"}\n{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"user_tool_result\",\"ts\":\"...\",\"tool_use_id\":\"toolu_xxx\",\"result\":{\"type\":\"text\",\"file\":\"path\"}}\n{\"v\":1,\"type\":\"assistant\",\"ts\":\"...\",\"id\":\"msg_xxx\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"...\"},{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"toolu_xxx\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{...}}]}\n```\n\nEvery line carries `\"v\":1` so readers can handle format evolution without needing to read the header first (e.g., when streaming or reading from an offset). When we add or change fields, we bump the version. Old readers ignore unknown versions; new readers handle both.\n\n**Entry types:**\n- `user` — user text messages: `type`, `ts`, `content`\n- `user_tool_result` — tool results: `type`, `ts`, `tool_use_id`, result metadata (`type`, `file`, `error`, `answers`)\n- `assistant` — assistant responses: `type`, `ts`, `id`, `content` (text + tool_use blocks only; thinking blocks dropped)\n\n**What's excluded (compared to raw agent transcripts):**\n- All `progress`/`system`/`file-history-snapshot`/`queue-operation` entries\n- Envelope fields (`parentUuid`, `cwd`, `sessionId`, `version`, `gitBranch`, etc.)\n- Thinking blocks + signatures\n- `message.usage` (already aggregated in `metadata.json`)\n- Tool result content text (only linkage metadata kept)\n- `progress:agent_progress` (subagent streaming)\n- Binary data…7343 chars truncated…mit, point the old name to the archived commit. Branches under `refs/heads/` may have branch protection rules or require fast-forward pushes, adding friction.\n\n**No interference with branch-based workflows.** Branch count limits, CI triggers on branch push, branch cleanup bots, branch protection rules — none of these apply to custom refs. The checkpoint data lives in a parallel namespace that's invisible to standard git tooling unless explicitly accessed.\n\n**Clean fetch semantics.** The default refspec (`+refs/heads/*:refs/remotes/origin/*`) only fetches branches. Refs under `refs/entire/` are never fetched unless the CLI explicitly requests them. This means `git clone` and `git fetch` never download checkpoint data by default — the CLI fetches exactly what it needs.\n\n**Consistent with v1 migration.** The current `entire/checkpoints/v1` lives under `refs/heads/` (it's a branch). Moving to `refs/entire/` in v2 creates a clean break — the CLI can detect which version is in use by checking the ref namespace.\n\n#### The solution: separate permanent and ephemeral data into different refs\n\nThree types of refs, each with different lifecycles:\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main                ← permanent: metadata + compact transcripts\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current        ← active generation: raw logs (rotates when full)\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001  ← archived generation (deletable wholesale)\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000002  ← archived generation (deletable wholesale)\n```\n\n**`/main`** is a single append-only ref with linear commit history. Works like today's `entire/checkpoints/v1` but stores only small, permanent data: `metadata.json`, `transcript.jsonl`, `transcript_hash.txt`, `prompt.txt`, `assets/`. Never needs cleanup. Grows slowly. Standard merge logic for concurrent pushes (same as today — the existing `fetchAndMergeSessionsCommon` works here).\n\n**`/full/current`** is a ref that accumulates raw `full.jsonl` files as commits. Each checkpoint adds its raw logs to this ref's tree using the same sharded layout (`<id[:2]>/<id[2:]>/0/full.jsonl`). When it reaches a configured capacity (e.g., N checkpoints or M megabytes), it rotates:\n\n1. Rename `current` → next sequential number (e.g., `0000000000001`)\n2. Create a fresh orphan `current`\n\n**`/full/NNNNNNNNNNNNN`** (archived generations) are frozen refs. Once all checkpoints in a generation age past the retention window, the entire ref is deleted — making the whole commit chain and all its raw log blobs unreachable in one operation.\n\n#### Why this works\n\n**Only 2-3 active refs at any time.** `/main` + `/full/current` + maybe one recent archived generation that hasn't been cleaned yet. Well within any hosting provider's limits, and invisible in the GitHub UI.\n\n**Cleanup = delete entire refs.** No tree surgery. No force-push. Just:\n```bash\ngit push origin --delete refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001\n```\nThe entire commit chain becomes unreachable. Git GC reclaims all the large blobs. No need to understand the tree structure or modify individual files.\n\n**`/main` is never touched by cleanup.** It's append-only and permanent. The existing merge logic handles concurrent pushes. No new complexity here.\n\n**No force-push anywhere.** Deleting a ref is not a force-push — it's a ref deletion. The `/main` ref uses standard fast-forward pushes (with merge recovery, same as today). The `/full/current` ref also uses fast-forward pushes. When it rotates, the old ref is renamed and the new `current` starts as a fresh orphan.\n\n**Generation boundaries align with cleanup.** The generation size should be chosen so that a single generation spans roughly the retention window. Example: if retention is 14 days and a solo dev produces ~2 checkpoints/day, a generation of 30 checkpoints means each generation covers ~2 weeks. Cleanup can delete an entire generation once the newest checkpoint in it is past retention.\n\n#### Ref structure in detail\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main → commit chain (linear history)\n  HEAD tree:\n    a3/b2c4d5e6f7/               # sharded by checkpoint ID (same as today)\n      metadata.json               # CheckpointSummary\n      0/\n        metadata.json             # Session metadata\n        transcript.jsonl          # Compact Entire Transcript Format\n        transcript_hash.txt       # SHA-256 of transcript.jsonl\n        prompt.txt\n        assets/\n          img-001.png\n      1/\n        metadata.json\n        transcript.jsonl\n        transcript_hash.txt\n    f1/9e8d7c6b5a/\n      ...\n\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current → commit chain (linear history)\n  HEAD tree:\n    a3/b2c4d5e6f7/               # same shard structure\n      0/\n        full.jsonl                # Raw agent transcript for session 0\n        content_hash.txt          # SHA-256 of full.jsonl\n      1/\n        full.jsonl                # Raw agent transcript for session 1\n        content_hash.txt\n    f1/9e8d7c6b5a/\n      0/\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2833},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T22:53:22.301Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"exec_command_end","call_id":"call_fE67SvCqg4OPse2VPHMxcm7w","process_id":"23566","turn_id":"019db23f-4ab9-7890-84ed-d8686c4395d3","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md"],"cwd":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/.worktrees/feat/checkpoints-v2-close-test-gaps","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md","name":"2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md","path":"/Users/pfleidi/entire/cli/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-03-19-checkpoints-v2-design.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","stdout":"","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"# Checkpoints v2: Transcript Compaction & Split Storage\n\n**Date:** 2026-03-19\n**Status:** Draft\n**Authors:** pfleidi + engineer 2 (TBD)\n**RFD:** [RFD-009 Transcript Compaction](../../../rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md)\n\n## Overview\n\nImplement checkpoints v2: dual-write to split `refs/entire/` refs (permanent `/main` + ephemeral `/full/*`), transcript compaction to Entire Transcript Format, generational rotation for raw logs, migration from v1, and cleanup of archived generations.\n\nTwo engineers work in parallel: **Workstream A** (v2 storage infrastructure) and **Workstream B** (transcript compaction). The workstreams converge for integration, validation, and v1 removal.\n\n## Scope\n\n### In scope\n\n- Dual-write (v1 + v2) behind `checkpoints_v2` strategy option\n- Entire Transcript Format compaction for all built-in agents\n- External agent compaction protocol extension (`compact-transcript` subcommand)\n- Updated read paths for all CLI commands\n- Generation rotation for `/full/current`\n- Push logic for custom refs under `refs/entire/`\n- v1 to v2 migration tool (`entire migrate`)\n- Cleanup tool for archived generations\n- Validation against test repos\n- Removing v1 support\n\n### Out of scope (deferred)\n\n- Pinning (`entire pin` / `entire unpin`)\n- Cold storage archive (S3/GCS)\n- `retain_raw_logs` compliance mode\n- GitHub Action for automated cleanup\n- Remote migration (`entire migrate --remote`)\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Every PR merged must result in a shippable product. New functionality is hidden behind strategy options.\n- Existing v1 functionality must not break during development.\n- v2 refs must not be pushed to production repositories until validated. A separate `push_v2_refs` safeguard controls this.\n- Validation uses dedicated test repositories, not production repos.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. Strategy Option & Feature Gating\n\nAll v2 functionality is gated behind strategy options in `settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"strategy_options\": {\n    \"checkpoints_v2\": true,\n    \"push_v2_refs\": false,\n    \"generation_retention_days\": 14\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `checkpoints_v2` (default `false`): enables dual-write to v2 refs alongside v1.\n- `push_v2_refs` (default `false`): enables pushing v2 refs to remotes. Exists as an additional safeguard during development — removed when v2 is validated and v1 support is removed.\n- `generation_retention_days` (default `14`): retention window for archived `/full/*` generations. The cleanup tool deletes generations whose newest checkpoint is older than this.\n\nThe implementer should decide how to expose these settings (e.g., convenience helpers in the `settings` package, direct map lookups, etc.).\n\n### 2. v2 Ref Structure\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main                <- permanent: metadata + compact transcripts\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current        <- active generation: raw logs\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001  <- archived generation (deletable)\n```\n\nCustom refs under `refs/entire/` are invisible in GitHub's branch UI, not fetched by default, and not subject to branch protection rules. This is a deliberate choice per the RFD.\n\nThe tree layout within each ref uses the same sharded path structure as v1: `<checkpoint_id[:2]>/<checkpoint_id[2:]>/`.\n\n### 3. Checkpoint Store Changes\n\nThe checkpoint store needs to support writing to two v2 refs in a single operation:\n\n- Metadata entries (metadata, prompt, optionally transcript.jsonl + transcript_hash.txt + assets) -> `/main` ref\n- Raw transcript entries (full.jsonl, content_hash.txt) -> `/full/current` ref\n\nThis is implemented as a separate `V2GitStore` type (in `checkpoint/v2_store.go` and `checkpoint/v2_committed.go`), independent from the v1 `GitStore`. The v2 store composes `GitStore` internally to reuse ref-agnostic helpers (tree surgery, session indexing, summary aggregation) while keeping v1 code untouched. The existing tree-splicing and sharding logic is reused.\n\nRead paths need v2 awareness — reading metadata from `/main`, raw transcripts from `/full/current` (with fallback to archived generations), then v1 branch.\n\nThe store also needs helpers for managing refs under `refs/entire/` (create, update, read, list by prefix).\n\n**Task checkpoints:** Task checkpoint data (`tasks/<tool-use-id>/checkpoint.json`, subagent transcripts) is small metadata and goes on `/main` alongside the parent checkpoint's metadata.\n\n**Shadow branches:** No changes. Temporary checkpoints are ephemeral and local-only.\n\n**`entire/trails/v1`:** No changes in this project.\n\n### 4. Dual-Write Condensation Path\n\nWhen `checkpoints_v2` is enabled, `CondenseSession` performs:\n\n1. Same extraction and metadata calculation as today\n2. If compaction is available for this agent: produce `transcript.jsonl` via the agent's compactor\n3. Split entries:\n   - **Main entries:** `metadata.json`, `prompt.txt`, and (if compaction available) `transcript.jsonl` + `transcript_hash.txt` + `assets/*`\n   - **Full entries:** `full.jsonl` (raw transcript) + `content_hash.txt`\n4. Wr…5165 chars truncated…another instance already rotated — skip. On push, the rotation conflict recovery merges local data into the latest archived generation (see section 9).\n\n### 8. Updated Read Paths\n\n**Resolution order** (shared `ResolveTranscript` helper):\n1. Check v2 `/main` ref for `transcript.jsonl` (compact format)\n2. Check v2 `/full/current` then archived generations for `full.jsonl` (raw)\n3. Fall back to v1 branch\n\nNote: `/main` never contains `full.jsonl`. When compaction is unavailable, the transcript is only on `/full/*`.\n\n**Per-command changes:**\n\n| Command | What it reads | v2 change |\n|---------|--------------|-----------|\n| `entire status` | session state (`.git/entire-sessions/`) | No change needed — does not read from committed checkpoints |\n| `entire explain` | metadata + transcript | Prefer `transcript.jsonl` from `/main`, fall back to `full.jsonl` |\n| `entire resume` | raw transcript | Read `full.jsonl` from `/full/*`, search archived generations |\n| `entire doctor` | ref consistency | Verify v2 refs exist and are consistent |\n| `entire clean` | archived generations | List, check retention, delete eligible refs |\n| `entire rewind` | shadow branches | No change (operates on temporary checkpoints) |\n\n### 9. Push Logic\n\nWhen `checkpoints_v2` AND `push_v2_refs` are both enabled, `PrePush` pushes all v2 refs using explicit refspecs (required since custom refs aren't covered by default fetch/push):\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main:refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current:refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/<archived>:refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/<archived>  (any local archived generations)\n```\n\nAll v2 refs are pushed to the same target as v1 checkpoints (the user's push remote or the configured `checkpoint_remote`). Custom refs don't have remote-tracking refs, so the \"has unpushed\" optimization isn't available — the CLI always attempts the push and lets git handle the no-op case.\n\n`PrePush` enumerates all local refs matching `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/*` and pushes each one. This ensures archived generations (created by rotation) reach the remote so that:\n- The cleanup tool can delete them remotely\n- `entire resume` on other machines can search archived generations\n\n**Merge recovery for `/main`:** `/main` contains only sharded checkpoint directories — no root-level files. Tree merges are always conflict-free. The recovery pattern is the same as v1: fetch the remote ref, flatten both trees, combine entries, create a merge commit, push again.\n\n**Merge recovery for `/full/current`:** Also conflict-free since `generation.json` is not written to `/full/current` (see section 7). Same tree-merge recovery as `/main`.\n\n**Rotation conflict recovery:** When a push of `/full/current` fails and the remote has archived generations not present locally, another machine has rotated. Instead of merging two divergent `/full/current` refs (which would duplicate checkpoint data across generations), the CLI merges local `/full/current` **into the latest archived generation**:\n\n1. `git ls-remote` to discover remote-only archived generation refs\n2. Fetch the latest one (cheap — most blobs already local)\n3. Tree-merge local `/full/current` into the archived generation's tree — git's content-addressing deduplicates shared shard paths automatically\n4. Update `generation.json` timestamps in the merged tree\n5. Push updated archived generation (fast-forward from the archive's previous commit)\n6. Adopt remote's `/full/current` as local\n7. Retry push of `/full/current`\n\nThis preserves the key GC property: each checkpoint's raw transcript exists in exactly one generation, so deleting a generation ref makes its unique blobs unreachable. The archived generation may slightly exceed the rotation threshold — acceptable.\n\nSee [Push Logic Design](2026-03-27-checkpoints-v2-push-logic-design.md) for the full specification.\n\n**Fetch logic:** Since `refs/entire/` refs are not fetched by the default refspec, the CLI needs explicit fetch-on-demand when reading from remotes (e.g., `entire resume` on a fresh clone). The v2 resume implementation fetches `/main` with treeless fetch (tree objects only, no blobs) and fetches `/full/*` refs on-demand when a transcript isn't found locally.\n\nDuring dual-write: v1 branch is also pushed (as today).\n\n### 10. Migration Tool\n\nMigration should be an explicit user action (not auto-triggered). The implementer should decide on the exact CLI surface (e.g., `entire migrate`, a flag on an existing command, etc.).\n\n**Process:**\n1. Read `entire/checkpoints/v1` branch tree\n2. For each checkpoint:\n   - Copy `metadata.json`, `prompt.txt` -> `/main`\n   - If compaction available for agent: generate `transcript.jsonl` + `transcript_hash.txt` and write to `/main`\n   - Copy `full.jsonl` + `content_hash.txt` -> `/full/current` (respecting generation rotation)\n   - Copy task checkpoint data if present\n3. Optionally delete v1 branch\n\n**Properties:**\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1916},"formatted_output":"","status":"completed"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T22:53:22.302Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_wlUQRMnPRLsCauhPtVR3YNSL","output":"Chunk ID: f63838\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 2659\nOutput:\n---\nname: brainstorming\ndescription: \"You MUST use this before any creative work - creating features, building components, adding functionality, or modifying behavior. Explores user intent, requirements and design before implementation.\"\n---\n\n# Brainstorming Ideas Into Designs\n\nHelp turn ideas into fully formed designs and specs through natural collaborative dialogue.\n\nStart by understanding the current project context, then ask questions one at a time to refine the idea. Once you understand what you're building, present the design and get user approval.\n\n<HARD-GATE>\nDo NOT invoke any implementation skill, write any code, scaffold any project, or take any implementation action until you have presented a design and the user has approved it. This applies to EVERY project regardless of perceived simplicity.\n</HARD-GATE>\n\n## Anti-Pattern: \"This Is Too Simple To Need A Design\"\n\nEvery project goes through this process. A todo list, a single-function utility, a config change — all of them. \"Simple\" projects are where unexamined assumptions cause the most wasted work. The design can be short (a few sentences for truly simple projects), but you MUST present it and get approval.\n\n## Checklist\n\nYou MUST create a task for each of these items and complete them in order:\n\n1. **Explore project context** — check files, docs, recent commits\n2. **Offer visual companion** (if topic will involve visual questions) — this is its own message, not combined with a clarifying question. See the Visual Companion section below.\n3. **Ask clarifying questions** — one at a time, understand purpose/constraints/success criteria\n4. **Propose 2-3 approaches** — with trade-offs and your recommendation\n5. **Present design** — in sections scaled to their complexity, get user approval after each section\n6. **Write design doc** — save to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md` and commit\n7. **Spec self-review** — quick inline check for placeholders, contradictions, ambiguity, scope (see below)\n8. **User reviews written spec** — ask user to review the spec file before proceeding\n9. **Transition to implementation** — invoke writing-plans skill to create implementation plan\n\n## Process Flow\n\n```dot\ndigraph brainstorming {\n    \"Explore project context\" [shape=box];\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" [shape=box];\n    \"Ask clarifying questions\" [shape=box];\n    \"Propose 2-3 approaches\" [shape=box];\n    \"Present design sections\" [shape=box];\n    \"User approves design?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Write design doc\" [shape=box];\n    \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\" [shape=box];\n    \"User reviews spec?\" [shape=diamond];\n    \"Invoke writing-plans skill\" [shape=doublecircle];\n\n    \"Explore project context\" -> \"Visual questions ahead?\";\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" -> \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Visual questions ahead?\" -> \"Ask clarifying questions\" [label=\"no\"];\n    \"Offer Visual Companion\\n(own message, no other content)\" -> \"Ask clarifying questions\";\n    \"Ask clarifying questions\" -> \"Propose 2-3 approaches\";\n    \"Propose 2-3 approaches\" -> \"Present design sections\";\n    \"Present design sections\" -> \"User approves design?\";\n    \"User approves design?\" -> \"Present design sections\" [label=\"no, revise\"];\n    \"User approves design?\" -> \"Write design doc\" [label=\"yes\"];\n    \"Write design doc\" -> \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\";\n    \"Spec self-review\\n(fix inline)\" -> \"User reviews spec?\";\n    \"User reviews spec?\" -> \"Write design doc\" [label=\"changes requested\"];\n    \"User reviews spec?\" -> \"Invoke writing-plans skill\" [label=\"approved\"];\n}\n```\n\n**The terminal state is invoking writing-plans.** Do NOT invoke frontend-design, mcp-builder, or any other implementation skill. The ONLY skill you invoke after brainstorming is writing-plans.\n\n## The Process\n\n**Understanding the idea:**\n\n- Check out the current project state first (files, docs, recent commits)\n- Before asking detailed questions, assess scope: if the request describes multiple independent subsystems (e.g., \"build a platform with chat, file storage, billing, and analytics\"), flag this immediately. Don't spend questions refining details of a project that needs to be decomposed first.\n- If the project is too large for a single spec, help the user decompose into sub-projects: what are the independent pieces, how do they relate, what order should they be built? Then brainstorm the first sub-project through the normal design flow. Each sub-project gets its own spec → plan → implementation cycle.\n- For appropriately-scoped projects, ask questions one at a time to refine the idea\n- Prefer multiple choice questions when possible, but open-ended is fine too\n- Only one question per message - if a topic needs more exploration, break it into multiple questions\n- Focus on understanding: purpose, constraints, success criteria\n\n**Exploring approaches:**\n\n- Propose 2-3 different approaches with trade-offs\n- Present options conversationally with your recommendation and reasoning\n- Lead with your recommended option and explain why\n\n**Presenting the design:**\n\n- Once you believe you understand what you're building, present the design\n- Scale each section to its complexity: a few sentences if straightforward, up to 200-300 words if nuanced\n- Ask after each section whether it looks right so far\n- Cover: architecture, components, data flow, error handling, testing\n- Be ready to go back and clarify if something doesn't make sense\n\n**Design for isolation and clarity:**\n\n- Break the system into smaller units that each have one clear purpose, communicate through well-defined interfaces, and can be understood and tested independently\n- For each unit, you should be able to answer: what does it do, how do you use it, and what does it depend on?\n- Can someone understand what a unit does without reading its internals? Can you change the internals without breaking consumers? If not, the boundaries need work.\n- Smaller, well-bounded units are also easier for you to work with - you reason better about code you can hold in context at once, and your edits are more reliable when files are focused. When a file grows large, that's often a signal that it's doing too much.\n\n**Working in existing codebases:**\n\n- Explore the current structure before proposing changes. Follow existing patterns.\n- Where existing code has problems that affect the work (e.g., a file that's grown too large, unclear boundaries, tangled responsibilities), include targeted improvements as part of the design - the way a good developer improves code they're working in.\n- Don't propose unrelated refactoring. Stay focused on what serves the current goal.\n\n## After the Design\n\n**Documentation:**\n\n- Write the validated design (spec) to `docs/superpowers/specs/YYYY-MM-DD-<topic>-design.md`\n  - (User preferences for spec location override this default)\n- Use elements-of-style:writing-clearly-and-concisely skill if available\n- Commit the design document to git\n\n**Spec Self-Review:**\nAfter writing the spec document, look at it with fresh eyes:\n\n1. **Placeholder scan:** Any \"TBD\", \"TODO\", incomplete sections, or vague requirements? Fix them.\n2. **Internal consistency:** Do any sections contradict each other? Does the architecture match the feature descriptions?\n3. **Scope check:** Is this focused enough for a single implementation plan, or does it need decomposition?\n4. **Ambiguity check:** Could any requirement be interpreted two different ways? If so, pick one and make it explicit.\n\nFix any issues inline. No need to re-review — just fix and move on.\n\n**User Review Gate:**\nAfter the spec review loop passes, ask the user to review the written spec before proceeding:\n\n> \"Spec written and committed to `<path>`. Please review it and let me know if you want to make any changes before we start writing out the implementation plan.\"\n\nWait for the user's response. If they request changes, make them and re-run the spec review loop. Only proceed once the user approves.\n\n**Implementation:**\n\n- Invoke the writing-plans skill to create a detailed implementation plan\n- Do NOT invoke any other skill. writing-plans is the next step.\n\n## Key Principles\n\n- **One question at a time** - Don't overwhelm with multiple questions\n- **Multiple choice preferred** - Easier to answer than open-ended when possible\n- **YAGNI ruthlessly** - Remove unnecessary features from all designs\n- **Explore alternatives** - Always propose 2-3 approaches before settling\n- **Incremental validation** - Present design, get approval before moving on\n- **Be flexible** - Go back and clarify when something doesn't make sense\n\n## Visual Companion\n\nA browser-based companion for showing mockups, diagrams, and visual options during brainstorming. Available as a tool — not a mode. Accepting the companion means it's available for questions that benefit from visual treatment; it does NOT mean every question goes through the browser.\n\n**Offering the companion:** When you anticipate that upcoming questions will involve visual content (mockups, layouts, diagrams), offer it once for consent:\n> \"Some of what we're working on might be easier to explain if I can show it to you in a web browser. I can put together mockups, diagrams, comparisons, and other visuals as we go. This feature is still new and can be token-intensive. Want to try it? (Requires opening a local URL)\"\n\n**This offer MUST be its own message.** Do not combine it with clarifying questions, context summaries, or any other content. The message should contain ONLY the offer above and nothing else. Wait for the user's response before continuing. If they decline, proceed with text-only brainstorming.\n\n**Per-question decision:** Even after the user accepts, decide FOR EACH QUESTION whether to use the browser or the terminal. The test: **would the user understand this better by seeing it than reading it?**\n\n- **Use the browser** for content that IS visual — mockups, wireframes, layout comparisons, architecture diagrams, side-by-side visual designs\n- **Use the terminal** for content that is text — requirements questions, conceptual choices, tradeoff lists, A/B/C/D text options, scope decisions\n\nA question about a UI topic is not automatically a visual question. \"What does personality mean in this context?\" is a conceptual question — use the terminal. \"Which wizard layout works better?\" is a visual question — use the browser.\n\nIf they agree to the companion, read the detailed guide before proceeding:\n`skills/brainstorming/visual-companion.md`\n"}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T22:53:22.302Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","call_id":"call_fE67SvCqg4OPse2VPHMxcm7w","output":"Chunk ID: 657efb\nWall time: 0.0000 seconds\nProcess exited with code 0\nOriginal token count: 3794\nOutput:\n# Checkpoints v2: Transcript Compaction & Split Storage\n\n**Date:** 2026-03-19\n**Status:** Draft\n**Authors:** pfleidi + engineer 2 (TBD)\n**RFD:** [RFD-009 Transcript Compaction](../../../rfds/rfd-009-transcript-compaction.md)\n\n## Overview\n\nImplement checkpoints v2: dual-write to split `refs/entire/` refs (permanent `/main` + ephemeral `/full/*`), transcript compaction to Entire Transcript Format, generational rotation for raw logs, migration from v1, and cleanup of archived generations.\n\nTwo engineers work in parallel: **Workstream A** (v2 storage infrastructure) and **Workstream B** (transcript compaction). The workstreams converge for integration, validation, and v1 removal.\n\n## Scope\n\n### In scope\n\n- Dual-write (v1 + v2) behind `checkpoints_v2` strategy option\n- Entire Transcript Format compaction for all built-in agents\n- External agent compaction protocol extension (`compact-transcript` subcommand)\n- Updated read paths for all CLI commands\n- Generation rotation for `/full/current`\n- Push logic for custom refs under `refs/entire/`\n- v1 to v2 migration tool (`entire migrate`)\n- Cleanup tool for archived generations\n- Validation against test repos\n- Removing v1 support\n\n### Out of scope (deferred)\n\n- Pinning (`entire pin` / `entire unpin`)\n- Cold storage archive (S3/GCS)\n- `retain_raw_logs` compliance mode\n- GitHub Action for automated cleanup\n- Remote migration (`entire migrate --remote`)\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Every PR merged must result in a shippable product. New functionality is hidden behind strategy options.\n- Existing v1 functionality must not break during development.\n- v2 refs must not be pushed to production repositories until validated. A separate `push_v2_refs` safeguard controls this.\n- Validation uses dedicated test repositories, not production repos.\n\n## Design\n\n### 1. Strategy Option & Feature Gating\n\nAll v2 functionality is gated behind strategy options in `settings.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"strategy_options\": {\n    \"checkpoints_v2\": true,\n    \"push_v2_refs\": false,\n    \"generation_retention_days\": 14\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `checkpoints_v2` (default `false`): enables dual-write to v2 refs alongside v1.\n- `push_v2_refs` (default `false`): enables pushing v2 refs to remotes. Exists as an additional safeguard during development — removed when v2 is validated and v1 support is removed.\n- `generation_retention_days` (default `14`): retention window for archived `/full/*` generations. The cleanup tool deletes generations whose newest checkpoint is older than this.\n\nThe implementer should decide how to expose these settings (e.g., convenience helpers in the `settings` package, direct map lookups, etc.).\n\n### 2. v2 Ref Structure\n\n```\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main                <- permanent: metadata + compact transcripts\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current        <- active generation: raw logs\nrefs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/0000000000001  <- archived generation (deletable)\n```\n\nCustom refs under `refs/entire/` are invisible in GitHub's branch UI, not fetched by default, and not subject to branch protection rules. This is a deliberate choice per the RFD.\n\nThe tree layout within each ref uses the same sharded path structure as v1: `<checkpoint_id[:2]>/<checkpoint_id[2:]>/`.\n\n### 3. Checkpoint Store Changes\n\nThe checkpoint store needs to support writing to two v2 refs in a single operation:\n\n- Metadata entries (metadata, prompt, optionally transcript.jsonl + transcript_hash.txt + assets) -> `/main` ref\n- Raw transcript entries (full.jsonl, content_hash.txt) -> `/full/current` ref\n\nThis is implemented as a separate `V2GitStore` type (in `checkpoint/v2_store.go` and `checkpoint/v2_committed.go`), independent from the v1 `GitStore`. The v2 store composes `GitStore` internally to reuse ref-agnostic helpers (tree surgery, session indexing, summary aggregation) while keeping v1 code untouched. The existing tree-splicing and sharding logic is reused.\n\nRead paths need v2 awareness — reading metadata from `/main`, raw transcripts from `/full/current` (with fallback to archived generations), then v1 branch.\n\nThe store also needs helpers for managing refs under `refs/entire/` (create, update, read, list by prefix).\n\n**Task checkpoints:** Task checkpoint data (`tasks/<tool-use-id>/checkpoint.json`, subagent transcripts) is small metadata and goes on `/main` alongside the parent checkpoint's metadata.\n\n**Shadow branches:** No changes. Temporary checkpoints are ephemeral and local-only.\n\n**`entire/trails/v1`:** No changes in this project.\n\n### 4. Dual-Write Condensation Path\n\nWhen `checkpoints_v2` is enabled, `CondenseSession` performs:\n\n1. Same extraction and metadata calculation as today\n2. If compaction is available for this agent: produce `transcript.jsonl` via the agent's compactor\n3. Split entries:\n   - **Main entries:** `metadata.json`, `prompt.txt`, and (if compaction available) `transcript.jsonl` + `transcript_hash.txt` + `assets/*`\n   - **Full entries:** `full.jsonl` (raw transcript) + `content_hash.txt`\n4. Write to v2 refs via `v2Store.WriteCommitted()`\n5. Also write to v1 via existing `store.WriteCommitted()` (dual-write)\n\nWhen compaction is NOT available (e.g., external agent without `compact_transcript`): `/main` gets metadata only (no `transcript.jsonl`), `/full/current` gets `full.jsonl`. Read paths fall back to raw transcript.\n\n**Interface between workstreams:**\n\nWorkstream A defines the interface that compaction must satisfy (input: agent + raw transcript, output: compact transcript bytes + assets). Workstream B implements it for each agent. The exact interface design is up to the implementers. Until B delivers, the condensation path skips compaction — `/main` has metadata only.\n\n**Stop-time finalization (`UpdateCommitted`):**\n\nThe existing codebase has an `UpdateCommitted` path in `manual_commit_hooks.go` that replaces transcript content on the v1 branch at agent stop time (when the agent finishes a turn after a mid-session commit). This finalizes a provisional transcript with the complete version.\n\nWhen `checkpoints_v2` is enabled, `UpdateCommitted` must also update v2 refs:\n- Update `full.jsonl` on `/full/current` with the finalized raw transcript\n- If compaction is available: update `transcript.jsonl` on `/main` with the finalized compact transcript\n- Continue updating v1 as today (dual-write)\n\nThis ensures `entire resume` reads the finalized transcript from v2 refs, not a stale provisional version.\n\n### 5. Compaction — Entire Transcript Format\n\n**Format:** Agent-agnostic JSONL as specified in RFD-009. Header line + conversational entries, every line carries `\"v\":1`.\n\n**Package structure:** The shared format types, compactor interface, and JSONL reader/writer need a home. The implementer should decide on package structure (e.g., a new `transcript` package or within existing packages).\n\n**Agent compactors:** Each built-in agent implements its own compactor co-located with the agent package (e.g., `agent/claudecode/`). The compactor understands the agent's native format and produces Entire Transcript Format output + extracted binary assets.\n\nWhere compactor implementations live (co-located with agent packages vs. centralized) is up to the implementer.\n\n**Agents requiring compactors:**\n- Claude Code (JSONL)\n- Gemini CLI (JSON messages array)\n- Cursor (JSONL variant)\n- OpenCode (JSON export)\n- Factory AI Droid (envelope format)\n- Copilot CLI (session format)\n\n### 6. External Agent Compaction Protocol\n\nSee RFD-009 \"Integration with external agent protocol\" section for the full specification.\n\nPlugins opt into compaction by declaring `\"compact_transcript\": true` in their `info` response capabilities.\n\n**Subcommand:** `compact-transcript --session-ref <path>`\n\nThe `--session-ref` argument is the absolute file path to the agent's transcript file. The plugin reads the raw transcript from disk, compacts it into Entire Transcript Format, and outputs JSON to stdout:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"transcript\": \"<base64-encoded Entire Transcript Format JSONL>\",\n  \"assets\": [\n    {\"name\": \"img-001.png\", \"data\": \"<base64>\", \"media_type\": \"image/png\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n- `transcript` (required): base64-encoded Entire Transcript Format JSONL. Base64 ensures byte-exact round-tripping and avoids nested JSON escaping issues.\n- `assets` (optional): extracted binary data. Each asset has a `name`, `media_type`, and base64-encoded `data`.\n\nIf the 10 MB stdout limit becomes an issue, a chunked version similar to `chunk-transcript` could be added later.\n\n**Fallback:** If the plugin does not declare `compact_transcript`, the CLI does not write any transcript to `/main` — only metadata (`metadata.json`, `prompt.txt`). The raw transcript and its `content_hash.txt` are written to `/full/current`. Read paths check `/main` for `transcript.jsonl` first, then fall back to `/full/*` for the raw transcript. No permanent `full.jsonl` on `/main` — this avoids leaving behind large, never-compacted files on the permanent ref.\n\n### 7. Generation Rotation\n\n**Trigger:** After writing to `/full/current`, count checkpoint shard directories in the tree (walk root shard prefixes, count second-level entries). If >= 100 (hardcoded default, may become configurable later):\n\n1. Walk the `/full/current` tree to compute `oldest_checkpoint_at` and `newest_checkpoint_at` from checkpoint session metadata\n2. Write `generation.json` to the tree root (timestamps only, no checkpoint list):\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"oldest_checkpoint_at\": \"2026-01-01T00:00:00Z\",\n     \"newest_checkpoint_at\": \"2026-02-15T00:00:00Z\"\n   }\n   ```\n3. Rename `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current` -> next sequential number (zero-padded 13 digits)\n4. Create fresh orphan commit with empty tree, point `/full/current` at it\n\n`generation.json` is **only written at archive time** — not during normal writes to `/full/current`. This keeps `/full/current` free of root-level files, ensuring conflict-free tree merges during push recovery (see section 9). The checkpoint list is not stored in `generation.json`; the tree structure itself is the authoritative source for checkpoint membership.\n\n**Concurrent rotation:** Re-read `/full/current` before rotating. If it's below the threshold, another instance already rotated — skip. On push, the rotation conflict recovery merges local data into the latest archived generation (see section 9).\n\n### 8. Updated Read Paths\n\n**Resolution order** (shared `ResolveTranscript` helper):\n1. Check v2 `/main` ref for `transcript.jsonl` (compact format)\n2. Check v2 `/full/current` then archived generations for `full.jsonl` (raw)\n3. Fall back to v1 branch\n\nNote: `/main` never contains `full.jsonl`. When compaction is unavailable, the transcript is only on `/full/*`.\n\n**Per-command changes:**\n\n| Command | What it reads | v2 change |\n|---------|--------------|-----------|\n| `entire status` | session state (`.git/entire-sessions/`) | No change needed — does not read from committed checkpoints |\n| `entire explain` | metadata + transcript | Prefer `transcript.jsonl` from `/main`, fall back to `full.jsonl` |\n| `entire resume` | raw transcript | Read `full.jsonl` from `/full/*`, search archived generations |\n| `entire doctor` | ref consistency | Verify v2 refs exist and are consistent |\n| `entire clean` | archived generations | List, check retention, delete eligible refs |\n| `entire rewind` | shadow branches | No change (operates on temporary checkpoints) |\n\n### 9. Push Logic\n\nWhen `checkpoints_v2` AND `push_v2_refs` are both enabled, `PrePush` pushes all v2 refs