{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T23:10:10.940Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"**Approaches**\n\n1. Recommended: add opt-in E2E settings helpers in `e2e/testutil`.\nThis keeps the current suite defaulting to v1-compatible behavior, and individual tests can switch a repo into `checkpoints_v2` or `checkpoints_version: 2` with one helper call. Lowest risk, easiest to roll out command by command.\n\n2. Add a second E2E execution mode driven by env vars.\nFor example, `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE=v2` could rewrite repo settings during `SetupRepo()`. This is good once coverage exists, but it’s a worse first step because it broadens the blast radius and makes failures less localized.\n\n3. Fork a parallel `*_v2_test.go` harness with custom setup wrappers.\nThis is explicit, but it duplicates setup and will drift quickly. I don’t recommend starting here.\n\nI recommend approach 1 first, then optionally adding approach 2 later once the v2 E2E scenarios are stable.\n\n**Design**\n\nAdd reusable scaffolding in `e2e/testutil` centered on repo-local settings mutation after `entire enable`. The scaffolding should provide small helpers like “enable dual-write v2” and “enable v2-only mode”, plus a couple of assertion helpers for checking `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main` and `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current`. That gives tests a standard way to opt into v2 mode without embedding JSON patching and git-ref inspection in each file. Does that direction look right so far?\n\nThe first consumers should be the existing resume-oriented E2E tests, because `resume` is already the most mature end-to-end flow and maps directly to the manual guide’s highest-signal scenarios. I would add a new v2-focused resume test file rather than rewriting the current resume tests in place. That keeps baseline coverage intact while we prove the scaffolding. After that, the same helpers can be reused for `explain`, then later for `attach`, and only after that for broader command coverage. Does that rollout order look right?\n\nI would explicitly separate two v2 modes in the scaffolding:\n- Dual-write mode: `strategy_options.checkpoints_v2=true`, for compatibility scenarios and v1 fallback checks.\n- V2-only mode: `strategy_options.checkpoints_version=2`, for stricter behavior where new writes skip v1 and pushes force v2 refs.\n\nThat distinction matters because several manual scenarios and current integration tests already treat them differently, and collapsing them into one helper would hide behavior we need to verify. If you agree, the implementation plan should treat these as separate first-class setup modes rather than a boolean toggle.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-21T23:10:10.940Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"**Approaches**\n\n1. Recommended: add opt-in E2E settings helpers in `e2e/testutil`.\nThis keeps the current suite defaulting to v1-compatible behavior, and individual tests can switch a repo into `checkpoints_v2` or `checkpoints_version: 2` with one helper call. Lowest risk, easiest to roll out command by command.\n\n2. Add a second E2E execution mode driven by env vars.\nFor example, `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE=v2` could rewrite repo settings during `SetupRepo()`. This is good once coverage exists, but it’s a worse first step because it broadens the blast radius and makes failures less localized.\n\n3. Fork a parallel `*_v2_test.go` harness with custom setup wrappers.\nThis is explicit, but it duplicates setup and will drift quickly. I don’t recommend starting here.\n\nI recommend approach 1 first, then optionally adding approach 2 later once the v2 E2E scenarios are stable.\n\n**Design**\n\nAdd reusable scaffolding in `e2e/testutil` centered on repo-local settings mutation after `entire enable`. The scaffolding should provide small helpers like “enable dual-write v2” and “enable v2-only mode”, plus a couple of assertion helpers for checking `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/main` and `refs/entire/checkpoints/v2/full/current`. That gives tests a standard way to opt into v2 mode without embedding JSON patching and git-ref inspection in each file. Does that direction look right so far?\n\nThe first consumers should be the existing resume-oriented E2E tests, because `resume` is already the most mature end-to-end flow and maps directly to the manual guide’s highest-signal scenarios. I would add a new v2-focused resume test file rather than rewriting the current resume tests in place. That keeps baseline coverage intact while we prove the scaffolding. After that, the same helpers can be reused for `explain`, then later for `attach`, and only after that for broader command coverage. Does that rollout order look right?\n\nI would explicitly separate two v2 modes in the scaffolding:\n- Dual-write mode: `strategy_options.checkpoints_v2=true`, for compatibility scenarios and v1 fallback checks.\n- V2-only mode: `strategy_options.checkpoints_version=2`, for stricter behavior where new writes skip v1 and pushes force v2 refs.\n\nThat distinction matters because several manual scenarios and current integration tests already treat them differently, and collapsing them into one helper would hide behavior we need to verify. If you agree, the implementation plan should treat these as separate first-class setup modes rather than a boolean toggle."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
