{"timestamp":"2026-04-22T18:14:06.135Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"I think your updated intent is better, and it changes the right abstraction boundary.\n\nFor E2E, the suite should model product behavior, not storage implementation branches. If a customer enables checkpoints v2, the normal E2E suite should exercise that mode by default. Later, when v2-only is the product mode, the suite should run in that mode the same way. From that perspective, my helper-first change is useful plumbing, but it is not the right end state.\n\nWhat I’d recommend is:\n\n1. Add a suite-level checkpoints mode setting for E2E setup.\nExamples:\n`legacy`\n`v2-dual-write`\n`v2-only`\n\n2. Make `SetupRepo()` apply that mode automatically.\nThat makes all normal E2E scenarios run under the selected product configuration, without each test opting in.\n\n3. Keep the helper functions only as low-level implementation utilities.\nThey should support the suite-level mode, not define the testing strategy.\n\n4. Continue keeping a small number of explicitly mode-specific tests only where behavior truly differs.\nFor example, assertions around v1 fallback are inherently dual-write-specific. But generic flows like `resume`, `explain`, and branch restore should run through the standard suite under whichever mode the suite is configured for.\n\nSo: your correction is sound, and I think we should pivot. The main caveat is rollout risk: flipping the whole suite immediately to v2 could create a large noisy failure surface. A practical path is to introduce the suite-level mode switch first, default it to current behavior, then run targeted E2E subsets in `v2-dual-write`, and later change the default once that is stable.\n\nIf you want, I can revise the plan around that model and treat the helper work from yesterday as internal scaffolding for a suite-wide `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` switch.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
