{"timestamp":"2026-04-22T18:18:31.452Z","type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"agent_message","message":"**Approaches**\n\n1. Recommended: suite-level `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE`.\n`SetupRepo()` reads one mode for the whole E2E run and configures `.entire/settings.json` accordingly.\nModes:\n`legacy`\n`v2-dual-write`\n`v2-only`\n\nThis matches your intent: the same customer-facing E2E scenarios run under the selected product mode.\n\n2. Per-test opt-in helpers as the primary mechanism.\nThis is what I started yesterday. It’s still useful internally, but it’s the wrong top-level testing model because it turns a product configuration into test-local implementation detail.\n\n3. Separate duplicated E2E suites per mode.\nThis would make drift likely and triple maintenance cost. I don’t recommend it.\n\nI recommend approach 1, with the helpers retained only as implementation utilities under the suite-wide mode switch.\n\n**Revised Design**\n\nAdd a suite-wide environment variable, likely `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE`, interpreted by `e2e/testutil.SetupRepo()`. That function should remain the single entry point for repo initialization, and it should patch settings immediately after `entire enable` so every downstream action in the scenario sees the selected checkpoints mode. Does that direction look right so far?\n\nMode behavior should be explicit:\n`legacy`: no v2 settings enabled.\n`v2-dual-write`: set `strategy_options.checkpoints_v2=true`, and likely leave `push_v2_refs` off unless a test specifically needs remote v2 refs.\n`v2-only`: set `strategy_options.checkpoints_version=2`.\n\nThat keeps the general suite customer-facing, while still allowing a small number of tests to layer in extra remote/push settings when the scenario actually depends on them. Does that separation between suite mode and scenario-specific extras look right?\n\nI would keep the current E2E tests structurally unchanged at first. Instead of writing many new v2-specific tests, I’d first make the existing `resume` and `resume_remote` flows runnable under all three modes. Then I’d add a small number of genuinely mode-sensitive assertions where behavior differs:\n`resume_remote` in `v2-dual-write` and `v2-only`\npossibly `explain` once we start using the suite-wide mode there\na few checks that the expected refs exist after checkpoint creation only where that validates customer-visible behavior around restore/fetch. Does that rollout order look right?\n\n**Revised Plan**\n\n1. Add suite-level `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` handling in `e2e/testutil.SetupRepo()`.\nUse the helper plumbing from yesterday internally, but stop treating it as the public testing model.\n\n2. Add parsing/validation for the allowed modes in `e2e/testutil`.\nFail early on invalid mode values so the suite doesn’t run with ambiguous settings.\n\n3. Update the E2E README or inline comments where needed so the suite’s checkpoints mode is documented as a run-level configuration.\n\n4. Adapt the most relevant existing E2E tests first.\nStart with `e2e/tests/resume_test.go` and `e2e/tests/resume_remote_test.go`, aiming for the same test logic to pass under:\ncurrent default behavior\n`v2-dual-write`\n`v2-only`\n\n5. Add only minimal mode-specific assertions.\nFor example, in remote resume scenarios, verify that the right restore/fetch path still succeeds in v2 modes. Avoid baking storage internals into every test.\n\n6. After that foundation is stable, decide whether to:\nchange the default suite mode to `v2-dual-write`, or\nkeep `legacy` default and run an additional CI lane for `v2-dual-write`, then later switch default to `v2-only`.\n\nIf you approve, the next implementation step should be step 1 only: wire `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` into `SetupRepo()` and stop for review.","phase":"final_answer","memory_citation":null}}
{"timestamp":"2026-04-22T18:18:31.452Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"output_text","text":"**Approaches**\n\n1. Recommended: suite-level `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE`.\n`SetupRepo()` reads one mode for the whole E2E run and configures `.entire/settings.json` accordingly.\nModes:\n`legacy`\n`v2-dual-write`\n`v2-only`\n\nThis matches your intent: the same customer-facing E2E scenarios run under the selected product mode.\n\n2. Per-test opt-in helpers as the primary mechanism.\nThis is what I started yesterday. It’s still useful internally, but it’s the wrong top-level testing model because it turns a product configuration into test-local implementation detail.\n\n3. Separate duplicated E2E suites per mode.\nThis would make drift likely and triple maintenance cost. I don’t recommend it.\n\nI recommend approach 1, with the helpers retained only as implementation utilities under the suite-wide mode switch.\n\n**Revised Design**\n\nAdd a suite-wide environment variable, likely `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE`, interpreted by `e2e/testutil.SetupRepo()`. That function should remain the single entry point for repo initialization, and it should patch settings immediately after `entire enable` so every downstream action in the scenario sees the selected checkpoints mode. Does that direction look right so far?\n\nMode behavior should be explicit:\n`legacy`: no v2 settings enabled.\n`v2-dual-write`: set `strategy_options.checkpoints_v2=true`, and likely leave `push_v2_refs` off unless a test specifically needs remote v2 refs.\n`v2-only`: set `strategy_options.checkpoints_version=2`.\n\nThat keeps the general suite customer-facing, while still allowing a small number of tests to layer in extra remote/push settings when the scenario actually depends on them. Does that separation between suite mode and scenario-specific extras look right?\n\nI would keep the current E2E tests structurally unchanged at first. Instead of writing many new v2-specific tests, I’d first make the existing `resume` and `resume_remote` flows runnable under all three modes. Then I’d add a small number of genuinely mode-sensitive assertions where behavior differs:\n`resume_remote` in `v2-dual-write` and `v2-only`\npossibly `explain` once we start using the suite-wide mode there\na few checks that the expected refs exist after checkpoint creation only where that validates customer-visible behavior around restore/fetch. Does that rollout order look right?\n\n**Revised Plan**\n\n1. Add suite-level `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` handling in `e2e/testutil.SetupRepo()`.\nUse the helper plumbing from yesterday internally, but stop treating it as the public testing model.\n\n2. Add parsing/validation for the allowed modes in `e2e/testutil`.\nFail early on invalid mode values so the suite doesn’t run with ambiguous settings.\n\n3. Update the E2E README or inline comments where needed so the suite’s checkpoints mode is documented as a run-level configuration.\n\n4. Adapt the most relevant existing E2E tests first.\nStart with `e2e/tests/resume_test.go` and `e2e/tests/resume_remote_test.go`, aiming for the same test logic to pass under:\ncurrent default behavior\n`v2-dual-write`\n`v2-only`\n\n5. Add only minimal mode-specific assertions.\nFor example, in remote resume scenarios, verify that the right restore/fetch path still succeeds in v2 modes. Avoid baking storage internals into every test.\n\n6. After that foundation is stable, decide whether to:\nchange the default suite mode to `v2-dual-write`, or\nkeep `legacy` default and run an additional CI lane for `v2-dual-write`, then later switch default to `v2-only`.\n\nIf you approve, the next implementation step should be step 1 only: wire `E2E_CHECKPOINTS_MODE` into `SetupRepo()` and stop for review."}],"phase":"final_answer"}}
